'''Call by Object Reference / Call by Sharing
Python uses call by object reference, also called call by sharing.
When an object is passed to a function, the function receives a reference to the same object.'''

#Example
def change(numbers):
    numbers.append(100)
my_list = [10, 20, 30]
change(my_list)
print(my_list)

#Output:[10, 20, 30, 100]

#Both my_list and numbers refer to the same list object. Therefore, modifying the list inside the function is visible outside.

#Modification vs Reassignment

'''This is the most important part.
Modifying the Object'''
def change(x):
    x.append(100)
numbers = [10, 20]
change(numbers)
print(numbers)

#Output:[10, 20, 100]


'''The existing object was modified.
Reassigning the Parameter'''
def change(x):
    x = 100
number = 10
change(number)
print(number)

#Output:10

'''Here, x is simply made to refer to another object. The original variable is not changed.

Easy Rule
MODIFY object
    ↓
Change can be visible outside'''


